  "evidence": "TEXT_E: \"modernise the NHS to deliver faster treatment, free at the point of use\"; pledges for \"20,000 extra nurses and 10,000 extra doctors\"; \"putting power, wealth and opportunity in the hands of the many, not the few\"; \"aim for full employment in every region\"; \"stronger support for children and families via tax and benefits\"; \"minimum wage rising to £4.20\"; \"tackle the causes of crime; empower local communities\"; commitments to fight \"global poverty and climate change.\" TEXT_F: \"A tax‑cutting, deregulating government\" with £8bn annual tax cuts; \"Can Work, Must Work\" conditional welfare; \"mandatory prison for dealers to under‑16s; honesty in sentencing\"; sovereignty measures (\"keep the pound; veto further transfers of power\"); emphasis on families/faiths via an Office of Civil Society; NHS patient choice including private providers; stronger national defence and constitutional reforms.",
  "reasoning": "The profile prioritises Care and Equality above Authority, Proportionality, Sanctity and Loyalty, so a programme centred on universal public services, redistribution/opportunity for the many, and social protection aligns better than one foregrounding sovereignty, punishment and market freedom. TEXT_E emphasises NHS expansion, welfare and family support, and equal opportunity, whereas TEXT_F stresses tax cuts, conditionality, national independence and tougher law‑and‑order, making TEXT_E the closer fit.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
